<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 3.1 Three-Dimensional Coordinate Systems.

  Definitions, theorems, summaries, and problem statements are kept;
  derivations and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes, each
  with a one-line statement of what has to be done.  Content mirrors
  ch-12-1.ptx of the full lecture notes; every xml:id here is prefixed
  with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figures: plotting a point in space is the skill being learned, so the
  two point figures become empty sets of axes to draw on.  The right-hand
  rule, the coordinate planes, the three planes through P(1,1,1), and the
  sphere are reference pictures rather than exercises, so they are
  reproduced in full, as in the lecture notes.  The distance figure keeps
  only the box and the two corners P_1 and P_2: the helper corners, the
  edge lengths and the diagonals are what the reader adds while deriving
  the distance formula.
  The figures answering parts A-G of the last example ARE its solution,
  so they are replaced by blank axes to sketch on.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-geom-3d-coordinates" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Three-Dimensional Coordinate Systems</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Locate a point <m>P(a,b,c)</m> in space using the rectangular
(Cartesian) coordinate system, and check that the axes are arranged
according to the right-hand rule.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Identify the three coordinate planes and the eight octants, and
recognize the surfaces described by equations such as <m>x = a</m>,
<m>y = b</m>, and <m>z = c</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Compute the distance between two points in space, and write the equation
of a sphere with a given center and radius.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Describe the region of space determined by a collection of equations and
inequalities, and use completing the square to recognize the equation of
a sphere.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-3d-coordinate-system">
<title>The Rectangular Coordinate System</title>

<p>
To locate a point in the plane we need two numbers; to locate a point in
space we need three. In this section we set up the rectangular
coordinate system in space, which is the setting for everything that
follows. We then measure distance, describe spheres, and practice
translating between an equation and the surface or region it represents.
</p>

<p>
We will study the Cartesian coordinate system, which is also known as
the <term>rectangular coordinate system</term>. We choose a point
<m>O</m> in space, called the <term>origin</term>, and three mutually
perpendicular lines through it, called the <term>coordinate axes</term>
and labelled the <m>x</m>-, <m>y</m>-, and <m>z</m>-axis. A point
<m>P</m> in space is then described by an ordered triple <m>(a,b,c)</m>,
where <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> are the signed distances from the
origin along the three axes.
</p>

<p>
On the axes below, mark off <m>a</m> units along the <m>x</m>-axis, then
<m>b</m> units parallel to the <m>y</m>-axis, then <m>c</m> units
parallel to the <m>z</m>-axis, and label the point <m>P(a,b,c)</m> you
reach.
</p>

<sidebyside width="60%">
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=1.05, >=stealth]
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (4.2,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,4.6,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,4.0) node[above] {$z$};
\node[above left] at (0,0,0) {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty set of three-dimensional coordinate axes labelled x, y, and z, meeting at the origin O, for plotting the point P with coordinates a, b, c.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
The three axes must follow the <term>right-hand rule</term>: if the
index finger of the right hand points along the positive <m>x</m>-axis
and the middle finger points along the positive <m>y</m>-axis, then the
thumb points along the positive <m>z</m>-axis.
<xref ref="fig-skel-3d-right-hand"/> illustrates this convention; the
square corner marks in that figure are a reminder that the three axes are
mutually orthogonal, so each pair of axes meets at a right angle. Every
coordinate system in this course is arranged this way.
</p>

<p>
The right-hand rule will return in
<xref ref="sec-skel-geom-cross-product"/>, where we use it in a second
form to determine the direction of the cross product
<m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> of two vectors.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-3d-right-hand">
<caption>
The right-hand rule. With the index finger of the right hand along the
positive <m>x</m>-axis and the middle finger along the positive
<m>y</m>-axis, the thumb points along the positive <m>z</m>-axis. The
square corner marks record that the three axes are mutually orthogonal:
each pair of axes meets at a right angle, even though the drawing on the
page must distort those angles.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A right hand with the index finger extended along the positive x axis,
the middle finger bent to point along the positive y axis, and the
thumb extended upward along the positive z axis. A small arc marks the
angle between the index and middle fingers. Three magenta square corner
marks at the origin, one for each pair of axes, indicate that the x, y, and z axes
are mutually perpendicular.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\usetikzlibrary{angles}
\definecolor{pinkskin}{RGB}{255, 219, 196}
\tikzset{thick vector/.style={->, thick}}
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
  \coordinate (O) at (1.0,0.7); % ORIGIN
  \coordinate (WT) at ( 2.9,-1.1); % WRIST TOP
  \coordinate (T1) at ( 2.3, 0.7); % THUMB
  \coordinate (T2) at ( 1.75, 2.3);
  \coordinate (T3) at ( 2.0, 3.1);
  \coordinate (T4) at (1.38, 3.15);
  \coordinate (T5) at ( 0.9, 2.3);
  \coordinate (T6) at ( 0.85, 1.2);
  \coordinate (T7) at ( 0.85, 0.2);
  \coordinate (I1) at (-1.1, 2.45); % INDEX
  \coordinate (I2) at (-2.9, 3.45);
  \coordinate (I3) at (-3.3, 2.9);
  \coordinate (I4) at (-1.5, 1.8);
  \coordinate (I5) at (-0.9, 1.1);
  \coordinate (I6) at (-0.9, 0.3);
  \coordinate (M1) at (-2.1, 0.9); % MIDDLE
  \coordinate (M2) at (-3.95,0.55);
  \coordinate (M3) at (-4.0,-0.15);
  \coordinate (M4) at (-2.3, 0.05);
  \coordinate (M5) at (-1.1, 0.20);
  \coordinate (R1) at (-1.9,-0.1); % RING
  \coordinate (R2) at (-1.8,-0.7);
  \coordinate (R3) at (-0.3,-1.5);
  \coordinate (R4) at ( 0.1,-1.7);
  \coordinate (R5) at ( 0.1,-1.0);
  \coordinate (R6) at (-0.5,-0.7);
  \coordinate (R7) at (-1.2,-0.3);
  \coordinate (P1) at (-1.9,-1.3); % PINKY
  \coordinate (P2) at (-0.8,-1.9);
  \coordinate (P3) at (-0.2,-2.1);
  \coordinate (P4) at (-0.05,-1.65);
  \coordinate (W1) at ( 0.4,-2.9); % WRIST BOTTOM
  \coordinate (W2) at ( 1.6,-3.5);

  % HAND
  \fill[pinkskin]
    (WT) -- (T6) -- (I5) -- (M5) -- (R2) -- (P2) -- (W2) to[out=25,in=-90] cycle;
  \draw[fill=pinkskin]
    (WT) to[out=120,in=-60] % THUMB
    (T1) to[out=120,in=-90]
