\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Now plot the point <m>(-4,3,-5)</m> on the axes below. (Watch the two
negative signs: decide before you draw which side of the origin each leg
goes, and which side of the <m>xy</m>-plane the point ends up on. Drawing
the dashed box whose corners are the origin and the point makes the three
coordinates easy to read back off.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="60%">
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=0.72, >=stealth]
\draw[->, thick] (-5.6,0,0) -- (2.6,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-1.2,0) -- (0,5.2,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,-6.6) -- (0,0,3.0) node[above] {$z$};
\node[above right] at (0,0,0) {$0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty set of three-dimensional coordinate axes, extended in the negative x and negative z directions, for plotting the point negative 4, 3, negative 5.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-3d-planes-octants">
<title>Coordinate Planes and Octants</title>

<p>
The three coordinate axes determine three <term>coordinate planes</term>.
The <m>xy</m>-plane is the plane containing the <m>x</m>- and
<m>y</m>-axes, and it is described by the single equation <m>z = 0</m>;
similarly the <m>xz</m>-plane is <m>y = 0</m> and the <m>yz</m>-plane is
<m>x = 0</m>. These three planes are shown in
<xref ref="fig-skel-3d-coordinate-planes"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-3d-coordinate-planes">
<caption>
The three coordinate planes: the <m>xy</m>-plane <m>(z=0)</m>, the
<m>xz</m>-plane <m>(y=0)</m>, and the <m>yz</m>-plane <m>(x=0)</m>. They
divide space into eight octants.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
Three shaded rectangles meeting at the origin at right angles represent
the three coordinate planes. The horizontal one is the x y plane where z
equals zero, and the two vertical ones are the x z plane where y equals
zero and the y z plane where x equals zero.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=0.95, >=stealth]
% xy-plane (z = 0)
\fill[orange!25, opacity=0.75] (-2.6,-2.6,0) -- (2.6,-2.6,0) --
                               (2.6,2.6,0) -- (-2.6,2.6,0) -- cycle;
% xz-plane (y = 0)
\fill[orange!45, opacity=0.65] (-2.6,0,-2.6) -- (2.6,0,-2.6) --
                               (2.6,0,2.6) -- (-2.6,0,2.6) -- cycle;
% yz-plane (x = 0)
\fill[orange!35, opacity=0.65] (0,-2.6,-2.6) -- (0,2.6,-2.6) --
                               (0,2.6,2.6) -- (0,-2.6,2.6) -- cycle;
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (3.6,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,3.6,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,3.4) node[above] {$z$};
\node[above left] at (0,0,0) {$O$};
\node[align=center] at (-1.5,-1.7,1.9) {$xz$-plane\\ $(y=0)$};
\node[align=center] at (0,1.9,1.9) {$yz$-plane\\ $(x=0)$};
\node[align=center] at (1.6,1.5,0) {$xy$-plane\\ $(z=0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
In the plane the two axes create four quadrants; in space the three
coordinate planes create eight <term>octants</term>. The
<term>first octant</term> is the one in which <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and
<m>z</m> are all positive.
</p>

<p>
From <xref ref="fig-skel-3d-coordinate-planes"/>, record the sign pattern
of the octant a point lies in. (Fill in the first octant from the
definition just given, then give the signs of the octant that lies
directly below it, and say how many of the eight octants have at least
one negative coordinate.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5.4);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.6) -- (15,3.6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,1.8) -- (15,1.8);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.25,5.15) {First octant, signs of $(x,y,z)$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.25,3.35) {The octant directly below it:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.25,1.55) {Octants with a negative coordinate:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box with three rows, for the sign pattern of the first octant, the sign pattern of the octant below it, and a count of the octants having a negative coordinate.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
More generally, equations such as <m>x = a</m>, <m>y = b</m>, and
<m>z = c</m> represent planes parallel to the <m>yz</m>-,
<m>xz</m>-, and <m>xy</m>-planes respectively. Each of these equations
places one restriction on a point of space and leaves the other two
coordinates free, so each describes a plane. Consequently a point can be
thought of as the intersection of three such planes: the point
<m>P(1,1,1)</m> of <xref ref="fig-skel-3d-three-planes"/> is the
intersection of the planes <m>x = 1</m>, <m>y = 1</m>, and <m>z = 1</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-3d-three-planes">
<caption>
A point as the intersection of three planes. The planes <m>x=1</m>,
<m>y=1</m>, and <m>z=1</m> meet pairwise in three lines, and all three
lines meet in the single point <m>P(1,1,1)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
Three shaded planes, one for x equals 1, one for y equals 1, and one for z
equals 1, intersect one another at right angles. Each pair of planes meets
in a red line: the planes x equals 1 and y equals 1 meet in a vertical
line, the planes x equals 1 and z equals 1 meet in a line running in the y
direction, and the planes y equals 1 and z equals 1 meet in a line running
in the x direction. All three red lines cross at the single common point,
marked in green and labelled P with coordinates 1, 1, 1.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=1.15, >=stealth]
% plane z = 1 (horizontal, pink)
\fill[purple!22, opacity=0.85] (-1.7,-1.7,1) -- (1.7,-1.7,1) --
                               (1.7,1.7,1) -- (-1.7,1.7,1) -- cycle;
% plane y = 1 (gray)
\fill[gray!30, opacity=0.7] (-1.7,1,-1.7) -- (1.7,1,-1.7) --
                            (1.7,1,1.7) -- (-1.7,1,1.7) -- cycle;
% plane x = 1 (blue)
\fill[cyan!25, opacity=0.7] (1,-1.7,-1.7) -- (1,1.7,-1.7) --
                            (1,1.7,1.7) -- (1,-1.7,1.7) -- cycle;
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (2.5,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.5,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.3) node[above] {$z$};
% lines of intersection of each pair of planes
\draw[red!70!black, thick] (1,1,-1.7) -- (1,1,1.7);
\draw[red!70!black, thick] (1,-1.7,1) -- (1,1.7,1);
\draw[red!70!black, thick] (-1.7,1,1) -- (1.7,1,1);
\fill[green!50!black] (1,1,1) circle (2pt);
\node[green!50!black, above right] at (1,1,1) {$P(1,1,1)$};
\node[purple!70!black] at (-1.5,-1.2,1) {$z=1$};
\node[gray!50!black] at (-1.5,1,-1.4) {$y=1$};
\node[cyan!60!black] at (1,1.5,-1.4) {$x=1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-3d-distance">
<title>Distance Between Two Points</title>

<p>
The distance between two points <m>P_1(x_1,y_1,z_1)</m> and
<m>P_2(x_2,y_2,z_2)</m> is denoted by <m>\left\|P_1P_2\right\|</m> and can
be computed with the Pythagorean theorem. As
<xref ref="fig-skel-3d-distance"/> shows, the segment <m>P_1P_2</m> is
the diagonal of a rectangular box whose edges are parallel to the
coordinate axes.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-3d-distance">
<caption>
The segment <m>P_1P_2</m> is the diagonal of a box with edges parallel to
the coordinate axes.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<description>
<p>
A rectangular box with edges parallel to the coordinate axes, drawn in a
three dimensional coordinate system. The point P one, with coordinates x
one, y one, z one, sits at the near lower corner of the box, and the point
P two, with coordinates x two, y two, z two, sits at the opposite upper
corner.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
% The box and its two opposite corners only: the helper corners, the edge
% lengths and the two diagonals are what the reader adds while deriving
% the distance formula.  Orientation matches the video in
% fig-skel-video-distance-formula: x to the right, y back, z up.
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(0.98cm,-0.16cm)}, y={(0.45cm,0.56cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=1.05, >=stealth,
                    lbl/.style={font=\small}]
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (5.4,0,0) node[below right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.6,0) node[above left] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,4.2) node[left] {$z$};
% the box, set out along x so the axes stay clear of it
\coordinate (P1) at (1.6,0.5,0.9);
\coordinate (P2) at (4.0,2.6,2.9);
\draw[cyan!65!blue, thin]
  (1.6,0.5,0.9) -- (4.0,0.5,0.9) -- (4.0,2.6,0.9) -- (1.6,2.6,0.9) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!65!blue, thin]
  (1.6,0.5,2.9) -- (4.0,0.5,2.9) -- (4.0,2.6,2.9) -- (1.6,2.6,2.9) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!65!blue, thin] (1.6,0.5,0.9) -- (1.6,0.5,2.9);
\draw[cyan!65!blue, thin] (4.0,0.5,0.9) -- (4.0,0.5,2.9);
\draw[cyan!65!blue, thin] (4.0,2.6,0.9) -- (4.0,2.6,2.9);
\draw[cyan!65!blue, thin] (1.6,2.6,0.9) -- (1.6,2.6,2.9);
% the two opposite corners
\fill[red] (P1) circle (2.2pt);
\fill[red] (P2) circle (2.2pt);
\node[lbl, red, below left, xshift=16pt, yshift=-5pt] at (P1) {$P_1(x_1,y_1,z_1)$};
\node[lbl, red, above right]  at (P2) {$P_2(x_2,y_2,z_2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Derive the distance formula from that box. (First mark the two helper
corners on <xref ref="fig-skel-3d-distance"/>: the corner
<m>A(x_2,y_1,z_1)</m>, reached from <m>P_1</m> along the box's base, and
<m>B(x_2,y_2,z_1)</m>, reached from <m>A</m> across it. Label the three
edge lengths, then apply the Pythagorean theorem twice: once in the base
triangle <m>P_1AB</m>, whose right angle is at <m>A</m>, and once in the
vertical triangle <m>P_1BP_2</m>, whose right angle is at <m>B</m>.
Substitute the first result into the second.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.6) -- (15,6.6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.3) -- (15,3.3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {Step 1, in the base: $\|P_1B\|^2 =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.3) {Step 2, up the vertical edge: $\|P_1P_2\|^2 =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.0) {Substituting, $\|P_1P_2\| =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the Pythagorean theorem in the base triangle, the Pythagorean theorem in the vertical triangle, and the distance formula that results from combining them.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
Record the formula you obtained here, so it is easy to find later.
</p>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-3d-distance">
<title>Distance in Space</title>
<p>
For <m>P_1(x_1,y_1,z_1)</m> and <m>P_2(x_2,y_2,z_2)</m>,
<md xml:id="eq-skel-3d-distance" number="yes">
\left\|P_1P_2\right\| =
\sqrt{(x_1-x_2)^2 + (y_1-y_2)^2 + (z_1-z_2)^2}.
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
The video in <xref ref="fig-skel-video-distance-formula"/> builds the box
of <xref ref="fig-skel-3d-distance"/> one edge at a time and applies the
Pythagorean theorem twice, in the same two steps. Use it to check the
derivation you just wrote.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-video-distance-formula">
<caption>
Deriving the distance formula in space by applying the Pythagorean
theorem twice: first in the base triangle <m>P_1AB</m>, then in the
vertical triangle <m>P_1BP_2</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-distance-formula"
       source="videos/DistanceFormulaProof.mp4" width="85%"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-3d-spheres">
<title>Spheres</title>

<p>
A <term>sphere</term> of radius <m>a</m> is the set of all points
<m>P(x,y,z)</m> whose distance from a fixed center
<m>P_0(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m> equals <m>a</m>, as in
<xref ref="fig-skel-3d-sphere"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-3d-sphere">
<caption>
A sphere of radius <m>a</m> centered at
<m>P_0 = P_0(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m>: the set of all points <m>P(x,y,z)</m> at
distance <m>a</m> from the center.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<description>
<p>
A shaded sphere with its center marked and a segment of length a drawn
from the center to a point P on the surface, illustrating that every
point of the sphere is the same distance a from the center.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
% drawn entirely in the picture plane so the ball, its centre and the
% radius are guaranteed to line up
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth]
% axes
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,2.9) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (3.6,-0.95) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-2.3,-1.05) node[below left] {$x$};
\node[above left] at (0,0) {$0$};
% the sphere
\shade[ball color=orange!30, opacity=0.92] (1.35,0.95) circle (1.05cm);
% equator, to help read it as a solid
\draw[orange!55!black, dashed, opacity=0.8]
  (1.35,0.95) ellipse (1.05cm and 0.3cm);
% centre and one radius
\fill[magenta] (1.35,0.95) circle (1.8pt);
\draw[magenta, very thick, dashed] (1.35,0.95) -- (2.09,1.69);
\fill[magenta] (2.09,1.69) circle (1.8pt);
\node[magenta] at (1.58,1.44) {$a$};
\node[magenta, above right=1pt] at (2.09,1.69) {$P(x,y,z)$};
% the centre is labelled briefly and pulled clear of the ball with a
% leader; its coordinates are given in the caption
\draw[gray, thin] (1.35,0.95) -- (0.45,0.12);
\node[magenta, below left=0pt] at (0.45,0.12) {$P_0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Turn that description into an equation. (Write the condition
<q>the distance from <m>P</m> to <m>P_0</m> is <m>a</m></q> using
<xref ref="eq-skel-3d-distance"/>, then square both sides.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6.6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.3) -- (15,3.3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.3) {The condition, before squaring:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.0) {Equation of the sphere:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, for the distance condition defining a sphere and for the equation of the sphere obtained by squaring it.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-3d-sphere">
<title>Equation of a Sphere</title>
<p>
The sphere of radius <m>a</m> centered at <m>P_0(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m> is
<md xml:id="eq-skel-3d-sphere" number="yes">
(x-x_0)^2 + (y-y_0)^2 + (z-z_0)^2 = a^2.
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-3d-complete-square">
<title>Recognizing a Sphere by Completing the Square</title>
<statement>
<p>
Describe the geometric surface represented by the following equation.
<md>
x^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 2x + 4y = 0
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Complete the square in <m>x</m> and in <m>y</m>; there is nothing to do
in <m>z</m>. Then match the result against
<xref ref="eq-skel-3d-sphere"/> and read off the center and the radius.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {Completing the square:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {Center:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (6.00,3.7) {Radius:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box with a large upper region for completing the square and a lower row split into a space for the center and a space for the radius.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-3d-describing-regions">
<title>Describing Regions in Space</title>

<p>
A single equation in <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m> normally describes
a surface, two simultaneous equations describe the curve where two
surfaces meet, and inequalities describe solid regions. The next example
collects several of these situations.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-3d-describe">
<title>Describing Equations and Inequalities Geometrically</title>
<statement>
<p>
Describe the geometrical meaning of the following equalities and
inequalities.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>x = 1</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x = 1,\; y = 2</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>1 \le x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \le 4,\; z \le 0</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + y^2 = 1,\; -1 \le z \le 1</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + y^2 = 1,\; z = 3</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(For each part, count how many of the three coordinates are restricted:
one restriction normally leaves a surface, two leave a curve, and an
inequality leaves a solid region. In parts <alert>E</alert> through
<alert>G</alert> notice which variable is left free, and remember that
what is a circle in the plane sweeps out a cylinder in space. Name each
object, and give its center, radius, or axis where it has one.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\foreach \y in {2,4,6,8,10,12} \draw[gray!35] (0,\y) -- (15,\y);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {A.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {B.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {C.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {D.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {E.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {F.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,1.7) {G.};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box divided into seven labelled rows, A through G, one for the description of each equation or inequality.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
Now sketch parts <alert>A</alert>, <alert>B</alert>, <alert>D</alert>,
and <alert>E</alert> on the four sets of axes below. (Shade a region
rather than outlining it when the answer is solid, and use a dashed
outline for a boundary surface that the region does not include.)
</p>

<sidebyside widths="24% 24% 24% 24%">
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, y={(0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=0.8, >=stealth]
\draw[thick] (-2.2,0,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (3.4,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[thick] (0,-2.4,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.4,0) node[below right] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,0,-2.4) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.4) node[above] {$z$};
\node[anchor=north, font=\small\itshape] at (0,0,-3.0) {A.};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty three-dimensional axes labelled A, for sketching the plane x equals 1.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, y={(0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=0.8, >=stealth]
\draw[thick] (-2.2,0,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (3.4,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[thick] (0,-2.2,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,3.4,0) node[below right] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,0,-2.2) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.2) node[above] {$z$};
\node[anchor=north, font=\small\itshape] at (0,0,-3.0) {B.};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty three-dimensional axes labelled B, for sketching the line where the planes x equals 1 and y equals 2 meet.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8, >=stealth]
\draw[->, thick] (0,-2.6) -- (0,2.6) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (2.6,-0.7) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-2.2,-1.0) node[below left] {$x$};
\node[above right] at (0,0) {$0$};
\node[anchor=north, font=\small\itshape] at (0,-3.0) {D.};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty axes drawn in the picture plane, labelled D, for sketching the half spherical shell.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8, >=stealth]
\draw[->, thick] (0,-2.6) -- (0,2.6) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (2.6,-0.7) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-2.2,-1.0) node[below left] {$x$};
\node[above right] at (0,0) {$0$};
\node[anchor=north, font=\small\itshape] at (0,-3.0) {E.};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty axes drawn in the picture plane, labelled E, for sketching the infinite cylinder of radius one about the z axis.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
Parts <alert>F</alert> and <alert>G</alert> are both built from the
cylinder of part <alert>E</alert>. Sketch them on the axes below, and say
in one line what changed. (In one part the free variable is restricted to
an interval; in the other it is pinned to a single value. Only one of the
two answers is a curve.)
</p>

<sidebyside widths="46% 46%">
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85, >=stealth]
\draw[->, thick] (0,-2.6) -- (0,3.0) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (2.6,-0.7) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-2.2,-1.0) node[below left] {$x$};
\node[above right] at (0,0) {$0$};
\node[anchor=north, font=\small\itshape] at (0,-3.2) {F. $x^2+y^2=1,\;-1\le z\le 1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty axes labelled F, for sketching the cylinder of radius one cut down to the slab between z equals negative one and z equals one.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85, >=stealth]
\draw[->, thick] (0,-2.6) -- (0,3.6) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (2.6,-0.7) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-2.2,-1.0) node[below left] {$x$};
\node[above right] at (0,0) {$0$};
\node[anchor=north, font=\small\itshape] at (0,-3.2) {G. $x^2+y^2=1,\; z=3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty axes labelled G, for sketching the circle where the cylinder of radius one meets the plane z equals 3.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>
